<figure class="cache-figure" data-persistence-recovery>
 <p class="figure-title">Which operations are in the durable prefix?</p>
 <div class="cache-controls"><label>Persistence policy <select data-policy aria-label="Persistence policy"><option value="rdb">RDB snapshot only</option><option value="everysec" selected>AOF · periodic fsync</option><option value="always">AOF · fsync before reply</option></select></label><label>Failure point <input data-step type="range" min="0" max="5" step="1" value="2" /></label></div>
 <p class="recovery-event" data-event></p>
 <ol class="recovery-log" aria-label="Ordered state changes"><li data-write="0"><b>12</b><span>Initial SET</span><small>saved</small></li><li data-write="1"><b>15</b><span>SET 15</span><small></small></li><li data-write="2"><b>∅</b><span>DEL</span><small></small></li><li data-write="3"><b>18</b><span>SET 18</span><small></small></li></ol>
 <div class="recovery-pair"><div class="recovery-node"><b>Before failure</b><strong data-memory></strong><small>In Redis memory</small></div><div class="recovery-node recovery-promoted"><b>After recovery</b><strong data-recovered></strong><small data-medium></small></div></div>
 <p class="explanation" data-explanation aria-live="polite"></p>
 <figcaption>Move the failure point across writes and completed fsyncs. Solid blocks have durable evidence; dashed blocks are written but not yet durable in this model; faded blocks have not happened. An initial snapshot and initial AOF state both contain 12. The hypothetical host failure loses all unflushed AOF bytes. This is not a simulation of killing only the Redis process, nor a timing guarantee for everysec. With “fsync before reply”, each displayed write includes its completed fsync.</figcaption>
</figure>
<script>
import{persistenceEvents,persistenceState}from'../../lib/caching/recovery.mjs';
document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-persistence-recovery]').forEach(root=>{const policy=root.querySelector<HTMLSelectElement>('[data-policy]')!,step=root.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('[data-step]')!;const render=()=>{const s=persistenceState(Number(step.value),policy.value);step.setAttribute('aria-valuetext',persistenceEvents[s.step]);root.querySelector('[data-event]')!.textContent=`Point ${s.step}: ${persistenceEvents[s.step]}`;root.querySelector('[data-memory]')!.textContent=String(s.memory??'Empty');root.querySelector('[data-recovered]')!.textContent=String(s.recovered??'Empty');root.querySelector('[data-medium]')!.textContent=s.policy==='rdb'?'From the initial snapshot':'Replaying the fsynced prefix';root.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-write]').forEach(el=>{const i=Number(el.dataset.write);el.classList.toggle('future',i>s.written);el.classList.toggle('durable',i<=s.durable);el.querySelector('small')!.textContent=i>s.written?'not yet':i<=s.durable?'durable':s.policy==='rdb'?'not saved':'unflushed';});root.querySelector('[data-explanation]')!.textContent=s.policy==='rdb'?'The only completed snapshot contains 12. Every later change is outside that snapshot, including the deletion.':s.written===s.durable?'All displayed writes are in the durable prefix. Replaying them reproduces the current value, including an empty key after DEL.':`The durable prefix ends before ${s.written-s.durable} later change${s.written-s.durable===1?'':'s'}. The model recovers ${s.recovered??'an empty key'}, not the current in-memory state.`;};policy.onchange=render;step.oninput=render;render();});
</script>
